messages:
noah: opened a small docs PR to clarify SOC2 audit retention/exports, add a Rust + Go SDK example for export + redaction, and fix a broken CLI anchor. PR: https://github.com/redwood-inference/docs/pull/812
tanya: quick read  looks sensible. two asks: 1) clarify that exports are point-in-time snapshots captured at request time and do not change scheduled deletions or tenant TTLs; 2) call out which roles can request exports/holds.
noah: proposed wording: 'Exports capture a point-in-time snapshot of audit data when requested and do not alter the configured retention TTL or pending deletions. Retention changes, exports, and legal holds may only be initiated by Organization Admins or users granted the Compliance role.'
mel: nit: add a sentence about export proofs — we log export checksum and requester.id so auditors can correlate. also include a short migration hint mapping legacy keys to the new schema (legacy.user_email -> principal.email).
legal-compliance: wording mostly ok. replace any instance of 'permanent' with 'indefinite' and add pointer to internal retention policy: https://internal/legal/retention-policy. add a one-line legal-hold lifecycle: 'Legal holds suspend scheduled deletions until released by Legal; releases are recorded in the audit trail.'
noah: updated text and added internal link + lifecycle line. added Rust + Go snippets and a short error example when redaction isn't configured (customers asked about failure modes). snippets below: 
```rust
// Rust example
let req = ExportRequest { tenant_id: "acme".into(), start: "2024-02-01".into(), end: "2024-02-28".into(), redact_pii: true };
let resp = client.export(req).await?;
```
```go
// Go example
req := &ExportRequest{TenantID: "acme", Start: "2024-02-01", End: "2024-02-28", RedactPII: true}
resp, err := client.Export(ctx, req)
```
Error example: 409 {"error":"redaction_not_configured","message":"PII redaction not enabled for tenant"}
cli-checker: PR 812 updated, link-check passed, staging build green.
noah: planning to merge tomorrow AM if legal-compliance confirms. thanks all :thumbsup:
